/* dreams-text.js — classic script; assigns to window.TD. No ES modules, so file:// works.
   Texts for dreams.html: angel:<sequence> and angeldigit:<0-9> (angel numbers), dream:<symbol> (the dream dictionary).
   136 texts. Written fresh for this demo; a live site edits or replaces them.
   Built by ../_astro-corpus-work/dreams/build-dreams.js. */
window.TD = window.TD || {};
window.TD.DREAM_TEXT = {"angel:000":"Plenty of people who keep spotting 000 read it as a blank page. Zeros stand for potential, the pause before something takes shape. It often catches the eye after a long stretch of work ends, or when the next step is unclear. Rather than a push to hurry, many take it as permission to rest and stay open. One small thing to try: write down three things you would attempt if nothing held you back, and leave the list on your desk for a week.",
"angel:111":"Noticing 111 again and again is often read as a sign that your attention is shaping things. One is the number of beginnings and of the self, tripled for emphasis. Someone might see it on the clock while mulling over a new class or a project they keep postponing. The common reading is to watch your thoughts, since they steer your choices. A small step: pick one idea you have been circling and do the first ten minutes of it today.",
"angel:222":"A run of 222 is usually taken as a message about balance and patience. Two points to partnership, give and take, and two things growing together at their own speed. You might see it while waiting on a reply, or while a friendship feels slightly lopsided. People often read it as an invitation to trust that slow progress still counts. A gentle action: reach out to one person you have been meaning to talk with, and listen more than you speak.",
"angel:333":"Seeing 333 over and over is commonly read as encouragement to express yourself. Three is the number of creativity, voice and company, so the sequence feels like a nudge to speak up or make something. Picture it showing up on a receipt the week you have been hiding a half-finished song or story. Readers often take it to mean you are not doing this alone. A small act: share one unfinished piece of your work with someone you trust.",
"angel:444":"Folks who keep running into 444 often hear it as a note about foundations. Four is the steady, built-to-last number: walls, routines, effort that adds up. It tends to get noticed when life feels shaky and the basics need tending, like a budget spreadsheet left unopened or a sleep schedule that has drifted. The usual reading is that careful, ordinary work is holding more up than you realize. One action: fix one small routine today and keep it going for a week.",
"angel:555":"Repeated 555 sightings are widely read as a signal that change is moving closer. Five is the restless number, tied to freedom, variety and a shake-up of the usual. People tend to notice it when a job, a move or an old habit already feels too tight. It is not taken as a verdict, only as a cue to stay flexible. A small thing to do: change one small piece of your routine this week, such as a different route home, and see how it feels.",
"angel:666":"Despite its spooky reputation, 666 is read in angel-number circles as a reminder about balance. Six leans toward home, care and the people you look after, and the tripled version asks whether giving and receiving are even. Someone might see it while stretched thin by family errands and work, with nothing left for themselves. Nothing about it is meant to frighten. A kind step: set aside one hour this week for something that is only for you.",
"angel:777":"Seeing 777 repeatedly is usually taken as a nod toward your inner life. Seven is the thinker's number, tied to reflection, study and quiet trust in your own judgment. It tends to get noticed after a stretch of effort, like finishing a course or a hard season, when it is time to look back and see what you learned. Some read it as congratulations. A small habit to try: take a ten minute walk without your phone and let your thoughts settle.",
"angel:888":"People who keep seeing 888 often read it as a sign about cycles of giving and return. Eight sits on its side as the infinity symbol, and it is tied to effort paying back and to using your influence well. You might notice it after taking on more responsibility at work, or after helping someone for a long time. The reading is reflective, not a promise. One small action: write down one thing your effort has built, and thank someone who helped.",
"angel:999":"Noticing 999 is commonly read as a sign that a chapter is closing. Nine is the last single digit, so it stands for completion, release and finishing what you started. It may appear while you clear out a closet, leave a long-held role, or let an old grudge cool. People take it as encouragement to let go gently instead of dragging things along. One small step: finish one lingering task, or give away one item you no longer use.",
"angel:1010":"Spotting 1010 is often read as a mix of fresh starts and open possibility. The one stands for beginning and the zero for the wide space around it, so together they hint at a start with room to grow. Think of someone who sees it just before sending an application, or while sketching a plan they have not shown anyone. A common takeaway is to begin before everything feels ready. A small action: take the first tiny step today, even if it is only a rough draft.",
"angel:1111":"Few sequences get as much attention as 1111, and many people read it as a prompt to make a wish and to notice what they are focusing on. Four ones stack the energy of beginnings, so it is seen as a moment to wake up to your own intentions. It may land during a quiet pause between meetings, with a decision pending. A simple practice: stop for a breath, name one thing you truly want, and write a single sentence about it.",
"angel:1212":"A repeat of 1212 is usually read as a call to start something while keeping your footing. The one pushes toward beginnings, the two asks for patience and cooperation, and alternating them suggests moving forward with care. It might show up when you are about to launch a project with a partner or a team. People take it as a nudge to trust the process and stay steady. A small step: ask one person to join you in a plan you have been carrying alone.",
"angel:1221":"Seeing 1221 is often interpreted through its mirror shape: one, two, two, one. The outer ones are about you and your starting point, and the inner twos about partnership and balance. Readers take it as a reminder that your own needs and other people's belong in the same conversation. Imagine seeing it while deciding how to split chores or plans with a roommate or partner. One small action: say plainly what you need, then ask what the other person needs.",
"angel:1234":"A climb like 1234 is read as a sign of steady progress, one step at a time. It counts upward the way a staircase does, so people take it as encouragement that you are moving, even if slowly. It may show up while you are learning a skill, saving up for something, or recovering your routine after a break. The reading is simple: keep going in order. A small action: write the next four small steps of one goal and do the first one today.",
"angel:1122":"Readers often connect 1122 to turning a vision into something built together. Two ones set the idea going and two twos bring in patience and partnership, so the pairing is read as an idea that grows with help. It could show up while you are planning a shared venture or a family project. The takeaway is usually to stay committed while working alongside others. A small step: tell one person about your plan and ask for one honest suggestion.",
"angel:1133":"Seeing 1133 repeatedly is often taken as a mix of initiative and creative voice. The ones start things and the threes give them expression, so people read it as a hint that your ideas want an audience. Think of someone who keeps noticing it while holding back a blog post, a pitch or a hobby they hide. The usual reading is to speak up and enjoy the process. A small action: share one idea this week, even in a short message to a friend.",
"angel:1144":"Folks who keep noticing 1144 tend to read it as beginnings that need a solid base. The ones point to fresh starts and the fours to routine, structure and patient effort. It may turn up when you are excited about a project but unsure how to keep it going past the first week. The reading is encouraging: a plan with steady habits behind it can carry the spark. A small action: choose a regular time each week for the project, and put it on your calendar.",
"angel:1313":"A recurring 1313 is often read as a message about creativity grounded in change. The one starts, the three expresses, and the pair repeated suggests a pattern of trying something new and sharing it. Some people notice it while shifting careers or hobbies, wondering whether their ideas are good enough. Whatever its spooky reputation, the angel reading is hopeful. One small step: make something simple, a drawing, a recipe or a short note, and show it to someone.",
"angel:1414":"Spotting 1414 often gets read as a call to build step by step on something you have just begun. The one carries the spark of a start and the four brings structure and effort, so together they suggest a plan with real legs. It might show up while you set up a new business idea, a study routine or a household system. Readers take it as a reminder that patience counts. A small action: draft a simple checklist for the first month and tick off one item.",
"angel:1515":"Noticing 1515 is commonly taken as a sign that a new direction is near and that it asks for flexibility. The one begins and the five loosens, so the pair reads as starting something while staying open to change. You might see it as you weigh a move, a new schedule or a different way of working. People take it as a nudge to follow curiosity. A small step: try one thing you have never done before this week, even something as modest as a new class.",
"angel:1717":"Readers who keep seeing 1717 often take it as encouragement to trust your own thinking while you begin something. The one starts and the seven turns inward, so the sequence is read as an idea worth reflecting on before acting. It could appear after you have been studying a choice for a while. The common reading is that your inner sense is worth listening to. A small action: spend ten quiet minutes writing what you already know about the decision.",
"angel:2020":"Seeing 2020 is usually read as a call to trust the quiet spaces. Two points to balance and partnership, and the zero adds open potential, so the pair suggests that patience leaves room for things to develop. It may appear during a slow stretch when you are waiting for news or for a relationship to settle. People take it as a reminder not to force things. A small step: pause a worry for one day and do something steady and calming instead.",
"angel:2121":"A repeat of 2121 is often read as cooperation leading into a new beginning. Two stands for partnership and patience, and one for the start, so alternating them suggests that a fresh chapter goes best with company. People notice it while joining a team, starting a shared hobby or reconnecting with someone. The reading is hopeful and modest. A small action: invite one person to try something new with you, and let them pick a detail of the plan.",
"angel:2222":"Four twos in a row, 2222, are often read as a strong reminder to hold steady and trust the slow build. Two is the number of balance, partnership and patience, so the repetition emphasizes giving things time. It tends to be noticed during waiting periods, like a long job search or a friendship that is mending. The reading is gentle: keep faith with your effort. A small action: do one patient thing today, such as sending a kind message and letting it be.",
"angel:2323":"Readers often connect 2323 to cooperation mixed with creative expression. The two asks for balance and the three adds voice and play, so the pair is read as getting along while still saying what you mean. It may show up while you are collaborating on a project, a band or a family event. People take it as a prompt to share the stage. A small action: ask a collaborator for their idea, then offer one of yours.",
"angel:3333":"Four threes in a row, 3333, are usually read as a loud nudge toward expression and support. Three is the number of creativity, voice and company, so the repetition feels like an invitation to say or make something. It could land when you have kept an idea to yourself for too long. Some read it as a sign that people around you are rooting for you. A small step: tell one friend about the thing you want to create.",
"angel:4444":"Seeing 4444 is commonly read as a strong reminder about foundations and steady work. Four stands for structure, routine and effort stacked up over time, so the repetition points to the unglamorous work that holds a life together. People notice it when they are building something, such as a household budget or a business. The reading is reassuring more than demanding. A small step: pick one chore you keep avoiding, like organizing paperwork, and give it twenty minutes.",
"angel:5555":"A run of 5555 is widely taken as a sign that change is moving through your life. Five stands for freedom, curiosity and shake-ups, so the repetition feels like a push to loosen your grip on routines that no longer fit. Someone might see it while a job, a home or a friend group is shifting. It reads as invitation, not alarm. A small action: write down one thing you would like to change and one small experiment to try.",
"angel:7777":"Four sevens in a row, 7777, are commonly read as an invitation to look inward and trust your own insight. Seven is the number of reflection, study and quiet knowing, so the repetition suggests it is a good moment to slow down and learn from recent experience. People often see it after a busy or confusing stretch. A small practice: spend fifteen minutes alone with a notebook and write down what the last few months have taught you.",
"angeldigit:0":"Zero is the open space in any number. In angel-number readings it stands for potential, wholeness and a fresh slate, and it tends to amplify the digits beside it. When several zeros stand out, people often read it as a pause before something begins, or as a reminder that you are already whole. A good response is to rest and stay curious instead of rushing to fill the quiet.",
"angeldigit:1":"One is the digit of beginnings. In angel-number readings it points to initiative, independence and your own focus, the spark that gets something started. When ones are prominent, people often take it as a nudge to act on an idea or to watch where your thoughts keep going. A simple response is to take one small first step toward something you have been postponing.",
"angeldigit:2":"Two is the digit of balance and partnership. In angel-number readings it points to cooperation, patience and trust that slow growth still counts. When twos are prominent, people often read it as a cue to look at a relationship, a collaboration or the way you share effort. A simple response is to listen well, ask for input, and give a situation a little more time.",
"angeldigit:3":"Three is the digit of creativity and expression. In angel-number readings it points to voice, play and the company of others, as well as the idea that you are supported. When threes are prominent, people often read it as a nudge to speak, make something or share an idea. A simple response is to try a small creative act and show it to a friend.",
"angeldigit:4":"Four is the digit of foundations. In angel-number readings it points to structure, routine, hard work and the steady habits that hold a life together. When fours are prominent, people often read it as a cue to tend the basics, like money habits, sleep or an overdue project. A simple response is to choose one practical task and finish it.",
"angeldigit:5":"Five is the digit of change and freedom. In angel-number readings it points to curiosity, adventure and loosening what no longer fits. When fives are prominent, people often read it as a sign to stay flexible, especially if a job, a home or a routine is shifting. A simple response is to try something new on a small scale and notice how it feels.",
"angeldigit:6":"Six is the digit of care and home. In angel-number readings it points to family, responsibility and the balance between looking after others and looking after yourself. When sixes are prominent, people often read it as a cue to check whether giving and receiving feel fair. A simple response is to do one kind thing for someone close, and one for yourself.",
"angeldigit:7":"Seven is the digit of reflection. In angel-number readings it points to study, inner wisdom and trust in your own judgment, and a pull toward quiet. When sevens are prominent, people often read it as a cue to slow down and learn from recent experience. A simple response is to spend a little time alone with a notebook and write what you have noticed.",
"angeldigit:8":"Eight is the digit of effort and return. In angel-number readings it points to influence, abundance of many kinds and the way cycles of giving come around. When eights are prominent, people often read it as a cue to look at how you use your energy and authority. A simple response is to notice what your work has built, and to share credit where it belongs.",
"angeldigit:9":"Nine is the digit of completion. In angel-number readings it points to endings, release and finishing what you began, as well as service to others. When nines are prominent, people often read it as a cue to wrap up a chapter and let go of what you are done carrying. A simple response is to finish one lingering task or clear out something you no longer need.",
"dream:water":"Clear, calm water in a dream often mirrors how settled your feelings are, while murky or choppy water points to something you have not sorted out yet. Maybe you are smiling through a week that actually stings. Water tends to follow emotion, so notice its mood in the dream: still, rushing, warm, cold. Ask yourself which feeling you have been carrying around without giving it a name.",
"dream:ocean":"Standing at the edge of a huge ocean, or floating in the middle of one, often reflects something bigger than you can see across: a life change, a loose end in a big decision, or a feeling with no obvious bottom. Maybe you just started a new job and every day teaches you something unfamiliar. Notice whether the sea felt inviting or frightening. What part of your life feels too large to measure right now?",
"dream:flood":"Rising water in a house or street usually mirrors feeling flooded in waking life: too many messages, too many obligations, or an emotion that has finally spilled over. Picture a full inbox, a packed family weekend, and a month of unspoken irritation arriving together. The dream is not a forecast. It just points at overload. What is one thing you could set down or say out loud this week to lower the water?",
"dream:rain":"Falling rain in a dream can stand for release, sadness, or a quiet cleanup after a long dry stretch. Maybe you finally cried at a movie after weeks of holding it together, and the dream picks up that same loosening. Pay attention to how the rain felt: soft and welcome, or cold and heavy. Is there a feeling you have been waiting for permission to let fall?",
"dream:fire":"Flames in a dream often mirror strong energy you are not sure how to handle: anger, passion, ambition, or a situation that feels close to boiling over. Maybe an argument at work keeps replaying in your head, and the dream gives it a visible heat. Fire can also clear ground for something new. Was it warming you or threatening you, and which part of your life matches that feeling?",
"dream:flying":"Soaring above rooftops usually mirrors a sense of freedom, or a wish for it. It may show up after you finish a hard project, make a decision you were putting off, or when you crave distance from daily pressure. How high you flew and how steady it felt can hint at your confidence right now. Where in your waking life would you like a wider view?",
"dream:falling":"A sudden drop in a dream often mirrors a feeling of losing your footing: a shaky plan, a conversation that went sideways, or something you cannot quite control. Think of the lurch you feel when a stair is missing in the dark. It is not a forecast of anything. It points at insecurity, so ask what in your life feels unsupported and what small step could steady it.",
"dream:teeth-falling-out":"Crumbling or loose teeth are one of the most common dream images, and in modern interpretation they usually reflect worry about how you come across: saying the wrong thing, looking unprepared, or feeling a loss of control. Picture dreading a toast at a family dinner. The dream is not a forecast. What situation lately has you nervous about being judged, and what would help you feel more ready?",
"dream:chased":"Running from someone or something in a dream often mirrors avoidance. Something in waking life keeps catching up with you: an overdue conversation, an unpaid worry, a feeling you keep outrunning. Maybe a text you have not answered for two weeks. Notice who or what was behind you, and whether you ever turned around. What would change if you stopped and faced it for five minutes?",
"dream:naked-in-public":"Finding yourself unclothed at school or work is a classic dream of feeling exposed. It often shows up when you are being seen more than usual: a new role, a big presentation, or a moment of honesty with someone. Others in the dream usually barely notice, which is worth remembering. Where in your life do you fear people can see more than you meant to show?",
"dream:an-exam":"Sitting down to a test you never studied for often mirrors a sense of being evaluated and underprepared, even when you are actually capable. Many people dream it years after school, usually during a stretch of performance reviews, new responsibilities, or self-doubt. Notice whether you felt panicked or just puzzled. Which situation lately makes you feel you must prove yourself, and what do you already know that you are overlooking?",
"dream:late":"Racing to catch a bus, a meeting, or a ceremony that is already leaving often mirrors a feeling of falling behind: in a career, a relationship, or a personal goal. Maybe you compare your timeline to a friend's and feel slow. The dream does not say you are behind, only that you feel it. Whose clock are you measuring yourself against, and is it really yours?",
"dream:house":"A home in a dream is often read as a picture of yourself, with rooms standing for different parts of your life. A tidy living room, a leaking roof, or a cluttered basement can each reflect how you feel about your habits, your family, or what you avoid looking at. Notice which part of the house held your attention. What room in your own life could use some care?",
"dream:a-new-room":"Discovering an extra room you never knew your home had is a common dream of untapped potential. It often arrives when you sense room for something new: a hobby, a skill, or a different way of spending your days. Think of finding a closet that turns into a studio. What was inside, and what part of your life might be asking for that kind of space?",
"dream:stairs":"Climbing or descending steps in a dream often mirrors movement between levels of your life: a promotion, a new stage, or going down into feelings you rarely visit. Steady steps suggest a pace that feels manageable, while endless or broken stairs can reflect effort that seems to go nowhere. Ask yourself whether you were heading up or down, and what waits at the next landing.",
"dream:elevator":"An elevator in a dream often reflects change that happens to you rather than through your own effort: a quick rise, a sudden drop, or being stuck between floors. It can mirror a situation where someone else seems to hold the controls, like a decision at work. Did you press the button or just ride? Where in your life would you like a firmer hand on the buttons?",
"dream:driving-a-car":"Behind the wheel in a dream often shows how you feel about steering your own life. A smooth, confident drive suggests you feel in charge of your direction, while a passenger seat or a confusing route can show doubt about who is deciding. Maybe you have been following someone else's plan at work. Notice the road, the speed, and who else was in the car. Where do you want to take the wheel?",
"dream:control-of-a-car":"Brakes that fail or a car drifting away from your steering often mirror a sense that a situation is moving faster than you can manage. Think of a schedule that fills itself, or a conflict you cannot slow down. The dream is not a forecast. It reflects stress and the wish for a firmer grip. What is one thing you could slow down or hand off?",
"dream:train":"A train in a dream often mirrors a path that feels set: a career track, a routine, or a relationship moving at its own pace. Boarding on time can reflect readiness, while watching it pull away may hint at a missed chance you keep thinking about. Maybe you commute and daydream about a different line. Do you feel like a passenger on this ride, and where is it heading?",
"dream:airplane":"Being aboard or watching a plane overhead often reflects big ambitions, a leap into something unfamiliar, or a desire for perspective. Turbulence can mirror nerves about a launch, such as a move, a new venture, or a difficult talk. Notice who flew the plane and how calm you felt. What goal of yours feels like it is taking off, and are you ready for the altitude?",
"dream:a-flight":"Watching your plane leave without you often mirrors a worry about a chance slipping away: a conversation you postponed, a decision you did not make in time. Many people have it when they feel pressure to keep up. It is not a forecast of travel trouble. Think about the gate, the clock, and what held you up. What opportunity are you afraid of missing, and is it truly gone?",
"dream:bridge":"Crossing a bridge in a dream usually reflects a transition: from one job, home, or season of life to another. A sturdy bridge can show confidence in the move, while a shaky or foggy one points to uncertainty. Think of the last time you stood between two chapters. Which side were you walking toward, and what would help you feel steadier on the crossing?",
"dream:road":"A long road stretching ahead often mirrors your sense of direction in life. A smooth highway can reflect clarity, while forks, potholes, or dead ends suggest choices that feel unresolved. Maybe you have been weighing two paths and cannot tell which suits you. Notice the surroundings and the pace. Is there a turn you have been avoiding, and what might you learn by taking a few steps down it?",
"dream:door":"Opportunities, thresholds, and choices are what an entry way commonly stands for in dreams. An open door can reflect welcome and possibility, while a closed one may show hesitation about stepping into something new. Picture standing outside a room where a meeting is being held, unsure whether to knock. What is on the other side? Which doorway in your waking life are you standing before, and what would make it easier to go through?",
"dream:locked-door":"A door that will not open often mirrors feeling blocked: a job you cannot reach, a conversation someone will not have, or part of yourself you have not yet explored. Maybe you keep pushing on a plan that needs a different approach. Notice how you reacted, whether you pushed, searched, or gave up. What would you do if the lock were not the only way in?",
"dream:key":"A key in a dream often reflects the sense that you already hold what you need to open something: a solution, a skill, or a bit of courage. Finding one may follow a stretch of feeling stuck, while losing one can mirror worry about access or trust. Imagine rummaging in your pocket at your front step. What do you think the key was meant to open?",
"dream:mirror":"Seeing your reflection, especially an odd or blurry one, often mirrors how you view yourself right now. It can bring up questions about identity, aging, a new role, or the face you show other people. Maybe you recently changed jobs and feel like a stranger in the part. Did the reflection look kind or critical? What would you say to the person in the glass if you met them today?",
"dream:snake":"A snake in a dream is a very old symbol with mixed readings. Many people link it to something hidden that is coming to the surface: a worry, a growing suspicion, or a stage of life being shed like old skin. Think of a nagging doubt about a coworker. The dream predicts nothing. Was the snake threatening or calm, and what in your life feels ready to change?",
"dream:dog":"A dog in a dream often reflects loyalty, companionship, and instinct. A friendly one may mirror feeling supported by someone close, while a growling one can show tension in a trusted bond or a fear of being let down. Maybe you have been wondering if a friend would show up for you. Notice how the dog behaved toward you. Where in your life do you want more faithful connection?",
"dream:cat":"A cat in a dream often mirrors independence, intuition, and comfort on your own terms. A relaxed one can reflect a wish for rest or self-reliance, while a scratching or hiding one may hint at a boundary you need or a subtle feeling you are ignoring. Think of a cat that ignores you until you stop trying. Where in your life do you want more freedom, or more quiet?",
"dream:horse":"A horse in a dream often stands for energy, drive, and freedom: raw power you could use or something you are trying to rein in. Galloping can reflect confidence and momentum, while a skittish or stuck horse may show frustration with progress. Maybe a project has finally picked up speed. Who was holding the reins? What part of your life feels strong enough to move, and are you steering it?",
"dream:bird":"A bird in a dream often carries a feeling of lightness, perspective, or a message you are sensing but cannot yet put into words. Songbirds may echo cheerfulness, while a trapped or fluttering bird can mirror a wish to get free of a situation. Think of watching a bird land on a windowsill during a stressful morning. What is the bird doing, and what in your life wants more room to fly?",
"dream:spider":"A web in the corner of a dream often mirrors something slowly built or quietly tangled in daily life. Some people wake uneasy, like a deadline has caught them mid-step. Others feel respect for patient, careful work. Maybe a group chat, a project and a family favor all pull on the same thread. What in your life feels both delicate and sticky right now?",
"dream:fish":"Swimming shapes under dark water often stand for feelings and ideas that have not fully surfaced yet. A bright fish can feel like a small good thing you almost missed, while a crowded tank can mirror a full schedule. Think of a half-formed idea you keep meaning to write down. Which thought has been circling just below the surface, waiting for you to notice it?",
"dream:wolf":"Something wild and watchful in a dream often reflects instinct, loyalty or a sense of being sized up. A lone wolf can echo feeling apart from the group at work or school. A pack can speak to belonging, or to pressure to keep pace. Picture standing at the edge of a party, unsure whether to join. Where do you want both freedom and company right now?",
"dream:bear":"A large, powerful animal in a dream can stand for strength you have not fully used, or for a situation that feels too big to handle. Some people feel protected by the bear, others cornered. Think of a conversation you keep avoiding because the other person seems enormous in your mind. What would change if you met that situation at its real size rather than the dream-sized version?",
"dream:lion":"Meeting a lion in a dream often touches on courage, pride and who holds the power in a room. It may mirror a confident side you are growing into, or someone whose presence fills every meeting. Perhaps you are about to speak up about something you usually let slide. Where are you being asked to take up more space, and what part of that feels exciting rather than frightening?",
"dream:insects":"Crawling or buzzing creatures in a dream often mirror small annoyances that pile up until they are hard to ignore. A swarm can echo a full inbox, a noisy household or worries that arrive all at once. Think of ten tiny tasks, none urgent, all irritating. Which minor problem have you been brushing aside, and what might happen if you dealt with it for ten minutes today?",
"dream:baby":"A baby in a dream often points to something new and tender in your life: a project, a habit, an idea or a part of yourself that needs care to grow. It can also mirror worry about being responsible for something fragile. Maybe you just started a small business or a new routine. What are you nurturing right now, and what does it need that you have not given it yet?",
"dream:pregnant":"Dreaming of being pregnant is usually read symbolically, as carrying something that is not ready to be seen yet. It might be a plan, a creative project or a big decision taking shape. It is not a forecast of anything in your body or your family. Think of a book idea you have told no one. What are you quietly growing, and how do you feel about sharing it?",
"dream:wedding":"A wedding in a dream often reflects commitment, a major transition or the feeling of being on display. It does not have to be about romance. It can mirror signing a lease, accepting a job or promising yourself something. Perhaps you are the one planning every detail while others simply attend. What are you being asked to say yes to, and are you saying it freely?",
"dream:ex-partner":"Seeing a former partner in a dream rarely means you want them back. More often, the person stands for a feeling or a pattern: being understood, being criticized, feeling free or feeling stuck. Maybe a recent argument echoed an old one. What quality did that relationship hold that you are missing or glad to have left, and where does it show up in your life today?",
"dream:dying":"Someone dying in a dream is usually read as a symbol of an ending, such as a job, a habit, a phase of life or a role you have outgrown. It is not a forecast about anyone. Think of closing out a chapter and feeling both grief and relief. What is finishing in your life, and what might be waiting to begin in its place?",
"dream:school":"Back in a classroom, often unprepared, many dreamers are really revisiting feelings about being judged, learning or measuring up. The setting returns when life asks for new skills, like a promotion or a move. It is like opening a test you did not know was scheduled. What are you still learning right now, and who do you imagine is grading you?",
"dream:work-or-the-office":"Dreams set at the office usually echo responsibility, performance and the way work follows you home. Missing a meeting or losing a file can mirror a fear of letting others down. Perhaps you answered messages at midnight and the habit slipped into sleep. Where is work taking more room than it deserves, and what would it look like to set one firm boundary this week?",
"dream:boss":"Someone in charge of you in a dream often represents authority, approval or your own inner critic wearing a suit. How you feel around that figure matters: nervous, defiant or proud. It may mirror an actual review coming up, or the way you judge your own results. Think of rereading an email three times before sending it. Whose approval are you chasing, and does it deserve that much of your energy?",
"dream:celebrity":"A famous face in a dream usually stands for a quality you notice and want, such as confidence, talent or attention. It can also mirror a wish to be seen or admired by others. Perhaps you watched an interview and felt a small pang. What does that person seem to have that you would like more of, and what is one small way to practice it yourself?",
"dream:stranger":"An unfamiliar person in a dream often represents a part of yourself you have not met properly, or a new influence entering your life. The mood tells a lot: welcoming, wary or curious. It may echo starting a new job or moving to a new town. What would this visitor say if they spoke, and what quality of theirs are you still getting to know in yourself?",
"dream:mother":"Your mother appearing in a dream often reflects care, comfort, old expectations and the way you look after yourself. The feeling matters more than the plot: warmth, guilt or distance. Maybe you caught yourself repeating a familiar phrase of theirs at the dinner table. Which of their habits do you carry gladly, which would you set down, and how do you want to mother yourself on a hard day?",
"dream:father":"Your father showing up in a dream commonly touches on authority, guidance, protection and the standards you absorbed growing up. It may also mirror how you handle rules and responsibility now. Think of hearing that advice in your head while making a big choice. Which lessons from your father still serve you, which feel like a weight, and what kind of steady support are you looking for today?",
"dream:friend":"A friend showing up in a dream often mirrors the qualities you associate with them, or something about how that friendship is going. A warm scene can reflect support, while a distant one may hint you miss them. Perhaps you have not replied to a message in weeks. What does this friend bring out in you, and is there someone you owe a call?",
"dream:money":"Cash in a dream tends to reflect self-worth, security and what you feel is valuable, rather than actual finances. Finding it can echo feeling resourceful, while counting or losing it can mirror anxiety about having enough. Think of checking your bank app twice before bed. What do you value most right now, and where do you feel you are giving more than you receive?",
"dream:your-wallet-or-phone":"Losing a wallet or phone in a dream often mirrors a fear of being cut off, unidentified or unprepared. These objects hold your identity and your connections, so their absence can feel like losing control. Think of patting your pockets at the door, heart racing. What would you feel without them, and what part of your sense of self depends too heavily on being reachable?",
"dream:phone-call":"A call in a dream often mirrors a wish to connect, or a message you have not delivered or received. Bad reception, wrong numbers or buttons that will not work can echo frustration at being misunderstood. Maybe you rehearsed a hard conversation in the shower. Who needs to hear something from you, and what is stopping you from saying it plainly?",
"dream:letter":"An envelope in a dream often stands for news you are waiting on, or words you have not said aloud. Opening one can feel hopeful or tense. Unread letters may mirror avoided emails or decisions. Think of a sealed envelope sitting on the counter for a week. What message are you either afraid to open or afraid to send, and what would help you face it?",
"dream:forest":"Trees pressing in on every side in a dream often reflect a stretch of life that feels unclear, dense or private. Some people feel lost, others peaceful and sheltered. It may echo a career change where the path has not shown itself yet. Is this a place to rest or to find your way out, and what small landmark could help you orient today?",
"dream:mountain":"A peak in a dream often stands for a big goal, a long effort or an obstacle that looks larger from the bottom. How you move on it says plenty: steady climbing, stalling, or reaching a view. Think of a degree, a savings plan or a training schedule. What summit are you facing, and what is the next single step rather than the whole climb?",
"dream:cave":"Deep, dark walls around you in a dream often points to withdrawal, inner exploration or something you have kept hidden, even from yourself. It can feel safe like a shelter, or closed in like a blocked path. Maybe you have been turning down invitations to stay home and think. What are you protecting in there, and is it time to bring a little of it into the light?",
"dream:garden":"Rows of plants in a dream often reflect what you are tending in your life: relationships, habits, projects or your own wellbeing. A flourishing one can mirror steady care, while weeds or a neglected bed may hint at something overdue. Think of watering a plant you forgot for a week. What have you been cultivating, and what needs pruning to make room?",
"dream:flowers":"Blooms in a dream often mirror appreciation, beauty and moments of opening up. Fresh ones can echo gratitude or a new friendship, while wilted ones may point to something you have let fade. Perhaps a neighbor handed you a bunch and you felt unexpectedly moved. What in your life deserves a little more attention and kindness, and who might enjoy hearing that you notice them?",
"dream:tree":"A tall trunk in a dream commonly represents growth, roots and the long view. A sturdy one can reflect stability, while bare branches may mirror a resting season. It might echo a family history you are exploring, or years spent building something slowly. Where are your roots strongest, which branch is reaching for new light, and what part of your life needs patient time rather than speed?",
"dream:moon":"Night light overhead in a dream often reflects moods, intuition and the quieter, cyclical side of life. A full moon can echo heightened feelings, while a thin crescent may mirror something still taking shape. Think of stepping outside at night and feeling oddly calm. What emotion has been rising and falling lately, and what might you notice if you gave it a few quiet minutes?",
"dream:sun":"Bright daylight in a dream often reflects vitality, clarity and a wish to be seen for who you are. It can mirror confidence returning after a rough stretch, or feeling spotlighted. Maybe you finally said yes to a plan you had doubted. What in your life feels warm and ready to grow, and where might you step out of the shade a little more?",
"dream:stars":"A sky full of them in a dream often reflects hope, wonder and the sense of something larger than daily worries. It can also mirror long-distance wishes, like a goal that feels far away yet guiding. Think of lying on a lawn and losing track of time. What dream are you holding onto, and what small action could point you a little closer to it?",
"dream:storm":"Dark clouds and heavy weather in a dream often mirror pressure building in waking life: an argument nobody has named yet, a deadline stack, a house full of tension. The storm can also be a release, the clearing that follows. Picture the week when everyone at work is short-tempered and you keep waiting for something to break. Ask yourself: what feels charged right now, and what would it take to let some of it out?",
"dream:earthquake":"Ground that shakes under your feet usually reflects a sense that something you counted on is no longer steady. It might be a job reshuffle, a move, or a long relationship changing shape. People often wake from it with their pulse up and a need to check what is solid. The dream is not a forecast, only a mirror. Ask yourself: what part of my life feels least stable, and what is still holding firm?",
"dream:snow":"A blanket of white outside the window can point to a quiet pause. Maybe you are tired of noise and part of you wants everything muffled for a while. Snow can also show feelings that have gone cold or a project that is covered over and waiting. Think of the stillness on a morning when school is canceled. Ask yourself: where do I need to slow down, and what is resting under the surface?",
"dream:ice":"Frozen ground or a sheet of ice in a dream often reflects feelings put on hold or a situation that feels stuck. Walking on it can mirror caution: you are moving forward but testing every step, maybe in a new job or a delicate talk with family. Ice also preserves things. Ask yourself: what in my life has gone rigid, and is it protecting something or just keeping me from moving?",
"dream:hair":"Long, tangled, thick or thinning, hair in a dream tends to be about how you present yourself and how you feel about your own vitality. Brushing it smooth can reflect a wish to get your life in order. A mess can mirror a day spent worrying how you came across. Think of fixing your reflection before a big meeting. Ask yourself: how do I want to be seen right now?",
"dream:haircut":"Scissors and falling strands often stand for change you are choosing or considering: a new look, a new role, letting go of an old version of yourself. A cut you did not want can echo worry about losing control over how others see you. Think of the lightness after a real trim. Ask yourself: what am I ready to shed, and who decided it was time?",
"dream:shoes":"Footwear in dreams tends to speak about direction and readiness. Tight or missing pairs can mirror feeling unprepared for the next step, like starting a class or a new role. Comfortable ones may point to confidence in how you are moving through things. Picture hunting for the second shoe while the door is closing. Ask yourself: where am I headed, and do I feel properly equipped for the walk?",
"dream:clothes":"Outfits in a dream often reflect the roles you wear and how comfortable you are in them. Wearing the wrong thing, or nothing at all, usually mirrors a worry about being judged or exposed, like showing up to a meeting feeling underdressed. A perfect fit can reflect ease with who you are. Ask yourself: where am I trying to look a certain way, and what would happen if I simply dropped it?",
"dream:food":"A table loaded with dishes, or an empty plate, often reflects what you feel fed by in waking life. Plenty can mirror satisfaction, shared meals and warmth. Hunger may point to a need that has not been met: attention, rest, creative time. Think of eating lunch over your keyboard for a month. Ask yourself: what nourishes me right now, and what am I quietly going without?",
"dream:kitchen":"Counters, stoves and cluttered sinks often stand for the everyday heart of home: how you care for yourself and the people near you. A busy, warm kitchen can mirror connection, while a messy or unfamiliar one can reflect feeling stretched by household demands. Think of the person who always ends up washing everything after a gathering. Ask yourself: who is doing the feeding here, and who feeds them?",
"dream:party":"Music, crowds and clinking glasses can show a wish for connection and recognition, or the social pressure of keeping up. A great party may mirror feeling included. Standing awkwardly by the snacks can reflect worry about fitting in, like arriving at a gathering where you only know the host. Ask yourself: do I want more company in my life, or a better quality of it?",
"dream:dancing":"Moving to music in a dream often reflects freedom, trust in your body, and a willingness to play. Dancing with a partner may mirror how you share the lead in a close relationship. Struggling to keep the rhythm can show a feeling of being out of step with a group or a schedule. Ask yourself: where in my life could I loosen up and follow the beat instead of the plan?",
"dream:singing":"Raising your voice in a dream can reflect a wish to be heard or a joy you have not yet let out. A strong, clear tone may mirror confidence. A cracked or silent one often echoes holding back in a meeting, or swallowing an opinion at the dinner table. Think of humming in the car when no one is listening. Ask yourself: what do I want to say out loud?",
"dream:swimming":"Moving through water, steady or struggling, often mirrors how you handle your feelings. Smooth strokes can reflect ease with emotion and flow. Heavy, tiring laps may point to a stretch where the demands feel bigger than the energy you have, like a month of caring for others and no downtime. Ask yourself: am I swimming toward something, or just trying to keep going, and would a rest on the shore help?",
"dream:drowning":"Water closing over your head is a vivid image, and it usually reflects overwhelm: too many tasks, too many feelings, too little air. It is not a forecast of anything real. Think of a week where every inbox, chore and family need arrives at once. If dreams distress you, talking to someone you trust can help. Ask yourself: what is weighing on me, and who could take a piece of it?",
"dream:boat":"A boat in a dream often mirrors the way you are carrying yourself through a stretch of life. Calm water and a steady hull can reflect confidence in your path. Drifting without oars may point to a lack of direction, like waiting on a decision that someone else controls. Ask yourself: who is steering, and is this the shoreline I actually want to head toward?",
"dream:island":"A small patch of land surrounded by water can stand for solitude, a refuge, or feeling cut off. For some it is a welcome retreat after a loud season. For others it mirrors isolation, such as working from home and talking to no one for days. Ask yourself: am I choosing this distance, or has it happened to me, and who might I wave to across the water?",
"dream:beach":"Sand, waves and open sky usually reflect a boundary between the practical and the emotional, and often a wish for rest. A calm shore can mirror relief and perspective. An empty or stormy one may point to feeling alone with your thoughts. Think of the first deep breath you take after a long drive to the coast. Ask yourself: when did I last give myself time with nothing to do?",
"dream:river":"Moving water in a dream often mirrors the pace of your life. A steady current can reflect flow, trust, and things unfolding naturally. Rapids might point to a hectic season, and a muddy, slow stretch can show feeling stuck. Think of the way a busy month carries you along without asking. Ask yourself: am I going with the current, against it, or standing on the bank deciding?",
"dream:rainbow":"A bright arc after rain tends to mirror hope after a hard patch, the sense that something is easing. It can also reflect a wish for beauty and meaning in ordinary days. Think of stepping outside after a rough week and noticing the light has changed. Ask yourself: what has recently improved that I have been too busy to notice, and who would I share it with?",
"dream:lightning":"A sudden flash across the sky often mirrors a quick insight, a shock, or a feeling that arrives all at once. It may reflect an idea that finally clicked, or an unexpected message that rattled you. Think of the jolt when an email changes your plans in a moment. Ask yourself: what has just become clear, and what am I now seeing differently?",
"dream:wind":"Gusts that push, whip or carry things away often reflect change moving through your life that you cannot fully control. A gentle breeze can mirror relief and fresh air, while a fierce one may show feeling blown around by other people's plans. Think of papers scattering across the table just as you sort them. Ask yourself: what is shifting, and what can I hold onto?",
"dream:lost":"Wandering without a map, in a city or a building you do not know, often reflects uncertainty about direction in waking life. A career turn, a move, or a decision with no clear answer can all trigger it. Think of circling the same block while you are late. Ask yourself: what choice am I facing, and what small landmark could help me find my bearings?",
"dream:searching-for-something":"Opening drawers and checking pockets in a dream usually mirrors a feeling that something is missing: an answer, a skill, a sense of purpose, or a person's attention. The object itself can be vague, which is part of the message. Think of patting your pockets for keys you cannot name. Ask yourself: what do I feel I am lacking, and have I defined exactly what I want?",
"dream:luggage":"Bags, packing and suitcases often reflect what you carry with you: responsibilities, old habits, worries from the past. A heavy or overflowing case can mirror feeling burdened. Leaving a bag behind may point to a wish to travel lighter. Think of lugging a duffel up three flights of stairs. Ask yourself: what am I hauling that no longer belongs on this trip?",
"dream:hotel":"Long hallways and rooms with numbers often reflect a time of transition, where you are between one chapter and another. It can feel temporary, like living out of a suitcase during a move or a job change. A comfortable room may mirror ease with not having everything settled. Ask yourself: what part of my life feels like a stopover, and where would I like to settle?",
"dream:hiding":"Crouching behind a door or under a bed often mirrors a wish to avoid something: a conversation, a deadline, a person, or attention you do not want. It can also reflect a need for privacy. Think of letting a phone call go to voicemail again. Ask yourself: what am I stepping away from, and is it time to face it or simply to rest?",
"dream:window":"Glass between you and the world can reflect watching life from a distance, or looking toward something you want. A clear view may mirror perspective and openness. A closed or fogged one often points to feeling shut off, like scrolling through other people's photos from your couch. Ask yourself: what am I looking at longingly, and what would it take to open the latch?",
"dream:attic":"Climbing up to a dusty, quiet space under the roof often stands for old memories, stored talents, and things you tucked away. Boxes may mirror feelings you have not sorted. Finding something treasured can reflect rediscovering a skill or a part of yourself. Think of unearthing a childhood drawing. Ask yourself: what have I put aside that I could use now?",
"dream:basement":"A step down into a dim lower level often reflects the parts of yourself that stay out of sight: old worries, buried feelings, habits you rarely look at. It can feel uneasy, though it is also where foundations sit. Think of the storage corner nobody visits until something breaks. Ask yourself: what have I been avoiding looking at, and what might it be holding up?",
"dream:bathroom":"Rooms for washing and privacy in dreams often mirror a need for cleansing, rest, or a moment alone. Finding one with no door or no privacy can reflect worry about being watched or exposed, like having a work call while the kids burst in. Ask yourself: where do I feel I never get a private minute, and what would let me reset?",
"dream:ring":"A band on a finger in a dream often reflects commitment, belonging, and promises, whether to a partner, a project or yourself. Losing it can mirror worry about a bond or a role slipping. Finding or wearing one may reflect a readiness to commit. Think of twisting a ring while you mull a big decision. Ask yourself: what am I committed to, and does it still feel true?"};
